Hi Igor,

The 88 and 120 sweep, which I said would go out either way and then left out of
three flashes before this one.

  oc     parity of DIV_ROUND_UP(oc,16)   the oc modulo 32 rule it replaced
  88     88 of 88 channels correct       hangs the block, 64 of 88 partial
  120    120 of 120 channels correct     hangs the block, 96 of 120 partial

Both counts discriminate, DIV_ROUND_UP even and oc modulo 32 nonzero, so the
two rules genuinely disagree there rather than agreeing by accident the way
1001 did. The partial figures are from runs that timed out, so treat the hang
as the result and the number as colour. 64 of 88 came back on two separate
boots.

So RK3576 needs the toggle at exactly the counts where yours does not, measured
at the shapes you suggested rather than at 56 alone.

I owe you a correction on the clamp, and it was my inference rather than your
data. I wrote that since the vendor userspace on the same silicon does not
clamp, the clamp had to be mine and there was no hardware behaviour left to
appeal to. The first half holds and the conclusion does not. The output stage
is

    byte = clamp(max(requant + L, 0) + offset, -128, 127) + 128

with the floor applied before the offset, so a negative requant is gone before
the offset can do anything about it. It is the silicon. The vendor does not
clamp because it lifts the accumulator first and takes the same amount back out
of the offset, which is the half I was missing. L is the output zero point,
which is the smallest lift that clears the floor, and with it the expression
collapses to clamp(requant + out_zp, 0, 255) with the offset a constant -128.
conv2d-cal went from 0 of 128 channels against the unclamped reference to 128
of 128, and every model whose zero point is zero came back byte identical to
emitting no lift at all.

The unclamped reference you pushed me towards has a second half I did not
expect, and it may touch your numbers as well. Scoring against the tflite
interpreter is not the same as scoring against the operation. Its requant
rounds a half away from zero, and computed offline against exact integer
arithmetic it reads high on 14.41 percent of a MobileNet depthwise's pixels,
while the board measures my driver differing from the interpreter on 14.38
percent of the same pixels, every one of them the other way. Against exact
arithmetic that layer is 99.98 percent of pixels identical. If the RK3588
figures you sent were against the interpreter too, some of what reads as error
there may not be.

